Now I understand the patterns. The `ManagedServiceIdentityType` converter reads a JSON object like `{"type":"SystemAssigned"}` and extracts the string value. So `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ManagedServiceIdentityType>($"{{{property}}}")` wraps the property in braces to make it a JSON object, then the converter reads it. I can replace this with directly reading `property.Value.GetString()` and constructing a `new ManagedServiceIdentityType(value)`.

Looking at the converter's `Read` method:
```csharp
using var document = JsonDocument.ParseValue(ref reader);
foreach (var property in document.RootElement.EnumerateObject())
{
    if (property.Value.ValueKind == JsonValueKind.Null)
        return default;
    else
        return new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
}
```

It reads the "type" property's string value. Since at the call site we already have the `property` which IS the "type" property, the value is just `property.Value.GetString()`. So we can replace with:
```csharp
type = new ManagedServiceIdentityType(property.Value.GetString());
```

For the `Write` (line 31), the converter does:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type");
writer.WriteStringValue(model.ToString());
```

So I can replace `JsonSerializer.Serialize(writer, ManagedServiceIdentityType, jOptions)` with:
```csharp
writer.WritePropertyName("type"u8);
writer.WriteStringValue(ManagedServiceIdentityType.ToString());
```

